==Latin==

===Etymology===
Pokorny<ref>{{R:ine:IEW|pages=36-37|volume=I|head=am(m)a}}</ref> suggests a derivation from {{der|la|ine-pro|*amma-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ama-|t=mother}}. Compare {{cog|la|amita|t=paternal aunt}}, {{cog|la|anus|t=crone}} and {{cog|goh|amma|t=wet nurse}}.

===Noun===
{{la-noun|amma<1>}}

# a [[nocturnal]] bird, perhaps the [[screech owl]]
#* {{c.|600}} Isidore of Seville, ''Etymologiae'', XII.vii.42:
#*: {{quote|la|Haec avis [viz. {{m|la|strix}}] vulgo '''amma''' dicitur, ab amando parvulos; unde et lac praebere fertur nascentibus.|t=This bird is commonly called ''amma'' [mum [[(?)]]], because it loves its young, and it is also said to offer milk to its hatchlings.}}

====Usage notes====
* Based on etymological evidence and the reference to milk in the Isidore quote, it is suspected that this word actually meant “mother”, along similar lines to {{m|la|atta|t=father}}.

====Declension====
{{la-ndecl|amma<1>}}

===References===
<references/>

